nfo.arxiv.org L38: \nL39: arXiv:1610.04769v3 [math.NA] 05 Apr 2018\nL40: # Optimal sampling rates for approximating analytic functions from pointwise samples\nL41: \nL42: Ben Adcock Affiliation: Department of Mathematics Affiliation: Simon Fraser University Affiliation: Canada    Rodrigo B. Platte Affiliation: School of Mathematical and Statistical Sciences Affiliation: Arizona State University Affiliation: USA    Alexei Shadrin Affiliation: DAMTP, Centre for Mathematical Sciences Affiliation: University of Cambridge Affiliation: United Kingdom\nL43: ###### Abstract\nL44: We consider the problem of approximating an analytic function on a compact interval from its values at $M+1$ distinct points. When the points are equispaced, a recent result (the so-called impossibility theorem) has shown that the best possible convergence rate of a stable method is root-exponential in $M$, and that any method with faster exponential convergence must also be exponentially ill-conditioned at a certain rate.\nL45: This result hinges on a classical theorem of Coppersmith & Rivlin concerning the maximal behaviour of polynomials bounded on an equispaced grid. In this paper, we first generalize this theorem to arbitrary point distributions. We then present an extension of the impossibility theorem valid for general nonequispaced points, and apply it to the case of points that are equidistributed with respect to (modified) Jacobi weight functions.\nL46: This leads to a necessary sampling rate for stable approximation from such points. We prove that this rate is also sufficient, and therefore exactly quantify (up to constants) the precise sampling rate for approximating analytic functions from such node distributions with stable methods. Numerical results – based on computing the maximal polynomial via a variant of the classical Remez algorithm – confirm our main theorems.\nL47: Finally, we discuss the implications of our results for polynomial least-squares approximations. In particular, we theoretically confirm the well-known heuristic that stable least-squares approximation using polynomials of degree $N<M$ is possible only once $M$ is sufficiently large for there to be a subset of $N$ of the nodes that mimic the behaviour of the $N^{\\mathrm{th}}$ set of Chebyshev nodes.\nL48: ## 1 Introduction\nL49: The concern of this paper is the approximation of an analytic function $f:[-1,1]\\rightarrow\\mathbb{C}$ from its values on an arbitrary set of $M+1$ points in $[-1,1]$. It is well known that if such points follow a Chebyshev distribution then $f$ can be stably (up to a log factor in $M$) approximated by its polynomial interpolant with a convergence rate that is geometric in the parameter $M$.\nL50: Conversely, when the points are equispaced polynomial interpolants do not necessarily converge uniformly on $[-1,1]$ as $M\\rightarrow\\infty$; an effect known as Runge’s phenomenon. Such an approximation is also exponentially ill-conditioned in $M$, meaning that divergence is witnessed in finite precision arithmetic even when theoretical convergence is expected.\nL51: Many different numerical methods have been proposed to overcome Runge’s phenomenon by replacing the polynomial interpolant by an alternative approximation (see [cite28†13 , cite29†26 , cite30†27 ] and references therein). This raises the fundamental question: how successful can such approximations be? For equispaced points, this question was answered recently in [cite30†27 ].\nL52: Therein it was proved that no stable method for approximating analytic functions from equispaced nodes can converge better than root-exponentially fast in the number of points, and moreover any method that converges exponentially fast must also be exponentially ill-conditioned.\nL53: A well-known method for approximating analytic functions is polynomial least-squares fitting with a polynomial of degree $N<M$. Although a classical approach, this technique has become increasingly popular in recent years as a technique for computing so-called polynomial chaos expansions with application to uncertainty quantification (see [cite31†14 , cite32†15 , cite33†23 , cite34†24 ] and references therein), as well as in data assimilation in reduced-order modelling [cite35†10 , cite36†18 ].\nL54: A consequence of the impossibility theorem of [cite30†27 ] is that polynomial least-squares is an optimal stable and convergent method for approximating one-dimensional analytic functions from equispaced data, provided the polynomial degree $N$ used in the least-squares fit scales like the square-root of the number of grid points $M+1$ [cite37†2 ]. Using similar ideas, it has also recently been shown that polynomial least-squares is also an optimal, stable method for extrapolating analytic functions [cite38†17 ].\nL55: ### 1.1 Contributions\nL56: The purpose of this paper is to investigate the limits of stability and accuracy for approximating analytic functions from arbitrary sets of points. Of particular interest is the case of points whose behaviour lies between the two extremes of Chebyshev and equispaced grids. Specifically, suppose a given set of points exhibits some clustering near the endpoints, but not the characteristic quadratic clustering of Chebyshev grids.\nL57: Generalizing that of [cite30†27 ], our main result precisely quantifies both the best achievable error decay rate for a stable approximation and the resulting ill-conditioning if one seeks faster convergence.\nL58: This result follows from an extension of a classical theorem of Coppersmith & Rivlin on the maximal behaviour of a polynomial of degree $N$ bounded on a grid of $M$ equispaced points [cite39†16 ]. In Theorem cite40†3.1 we extend the lower bound proved in [cite39†16 ] to arbitrary sets of points. We next present an abstract impossibility result (Lemma cite41†4.1 ) valid for arbitrary sets of points.\nL59: To illustrate this result in a concrete setting, we then specialize to the case of nodes which are equidistributed with respect to modified Jacobi weight functions. Such weight functions take the form\nL60:  | $$\\mu(x)=g(x)(1-x)^{\\alpha}(1+x)^{\\beta},$$  |\nL61: \nL62: where $\\alpha,\\beta>-1$ and $c_{1}\\leq g(x)\\leq c_{2}$ almost everywhere, and include equispaced ($\\mu(x)=\\frac{1}{2}$) and Chebyshev ($\\mu(x)=\\frac{1}{\\pi\\sqrt{1-x^{2}}}$) weight functions as special cases. In our main result, Theorem cite42†4.2 , we prove an extended impossibility theorem for the corresponding nodes. Generalizing [cite30†27 ], two important consequences of this theorem are as follows:\nL63: \nL64:   * (i)\nL65: If $\\gamma=\\max\\{\\alpha,\\beta\\}>-1/2$, any method that converges exponentially fast in $M$ with geometric rate, i.e. the error decays like $\\mathcal{O}(\\rho^{-M})$ for some $\\rho>1$, must also be exponentially ill-conditioned in $M$ at a geometric rate.\nL66: \nL67:   * (ii)\nL68: \nL69: The best possible convergence rate for a stable approximation is subgeometric with index $\\nu=\\frac{1}{2(\\gamma+1)}$. That is, the error is at best $\\mathcal{O}(\\rho^{-M^{\\nu}})$ for some $\\rho>1$ as $M\\rightarrow\\infty$.\nL70: We also give a full characterization of the trade-off between exponential ill-conditioning and exponential convergence at subgeometric rates lying strictly between $\\nu=\\frac{1}{2(\\gamma+1)}$ and $\\nu=1$.\nL71: Although not a result about polynomials per se, this theorem is closely related to the behaviour of discrete least-squares fitting with polynomials of degree $N<M$. Indeed, the quantity we estimate in Theorem cite40†3.1 is equivalent (up to a factor of $\\sqrt{M}$) to the infinity-norm condition number of such an approximation. By using polynomial least-squares as our method, in Proposition cite43†5.5 we show that the rate described in (ii) is not only necessary for stable recovery but also sufficient.\nL72: Specifically, when the polynomial degree is chosen as\nL73:  | $$N\\asymp M^{\\nu},\\hskip 20.00003pt\\nu=\\frac{1}{2(\\gamma+1)},$$  |  | (1.1)\nL74: \nL75: the polynomial least-squares approximation is stable and converges like $\\mathcal{O}(\\rho^{-M^{\\nu}})$ for all functions analytic in an appropriate complex region. The fact that such a method is optimal in view of the generalized impossibility theorem goes some way towards justifying the popularity of discrete least-squares techniques.\nL76: Besides these results, in §cite21†6 we also introduce an algorithm for computing the maximal polynomial for an arbitrary set of nodes. This algorithm, which is based on a result of Schönhage [cite44†30 ], is a variant of the classical Remez algorithm for computing best uniform approximations (see, for example, [cite45†25 , cite46†28 ]). We use this algorithm to present numerical results in the paper confirming our various theoretical estimates.\nL77: Finally, let us note that one particular consequence of our results is a confirmation of a popular heuristic for polynomial least-squares approximation (see, for example, [cite47†12 ]). Namely, the number of nonequispaced nodes required to stably recover a polynomial approximation of degree $N$ is of the same order as the number of nodes required for there to exist a subset of those nodes of size $N+1$ which mimics the distribution of the Chebyshev nodes $\\{\\cos(n\\pi/N)\\}^{N}_{n=0}$.\nL78: In Proposition cite48†5.6 we show that the same sufficient condition for boundedness of the maximal polynomial also implies the existence of a subset of $N$ of the original $M+1$ nodes which interlace the Chebyshev nodes.\nL79: In particular, for nodes that are equidistributed according to a modified Jacobi weight function one has this interlacing property whenever the condition $M\\asymp N^{2(\\gamma+1)}$ holds, which is identical to the necessary and sufficient condition (cite49†1.1 ) for stability of the least-squares approximation.\nL80: ## 2 Preliminaries\nL81: Our focus in this paper is on functions defined on compact intervals, which we normalize to the unit interval $[-1,1]$. Unless otherwise stated, all functions will be complex-valued, and in particular, polynomials may have complex coefficients. Throughout the paper $-1=x_{0}<\\ldots<x_{M}=1$ will denote the nodes at which a function $f:[-1,1]\\rightarrow\\mathbb{C}$ is sampled. We include both endpoints $x=\\pm 1$ in this set for convenience.\nL82: All results we prove remain valid (with minor alterations) for the case when either or both endpoints is excluded.\nL83: We require several further pieces of notation. Where necessary throughout the paper $N\\leq M$ will denote the degree of a polynomial. We write $\\|f\\|_{\\infty}$ for the uniform norm of a function $f\\in C([-1,1])$ and $\\|f\\|_{M,\\infty}=\\max_{m=1,\\ldots,M}|f(x_{m})|$ for the discrete uniform semi-norm of $f$ on the grid of points. We write $\\langle\\cdot,\\cdot\\rangle$ for the Euclidean inner product on $L^{2}(-1,1)$ and ${\\left\\|\\cdot\\right\\|}_{2}$ for the Euclidean norm.\nL84: Correspondingly, we let $\\langle f,g\\rangle_{M}=\\frac{2}{M+1}\\sum^{M}_{m=0}f(x_{m})\\overline{g(x_{m})}$ and $\\|f\\|_{M,2}=\\sqrt{\\langle f,f\\rangle_{M}}$ be the discrete semi-inner product and semi-norm respectively.\nL85: We will say that a sequence $a_{n}$ converges to zero exponentially fast if $|a_{n}|=\\mathcal{O}\\left(\\rho^{-n^{r}}\\right)$ for some $\\rho>1$ and $r>0$. If $r=1$ then we say the convergence is geometric, and if $0<r<1$ or $r>1$ then it is subgeometric or supergeometric respectively. When $r=1/2$ we also refer to this convergence as root-exponential.\nL86: Given two nonnegative sequences $a_{n}$ and $b_{n}$ we write $a_{n}\\asymp b_{n}$ as $n\\rightarrow\\infty$ if there exist constants $c_{1},c_{2}>0$ such that $c_{1}b_{n}\\leq a_{n}\\leq c_{2}b_{n}$ for all large $n$. Finally, we will on occasion use the notation $A\\lesssim B$ to mean that there exists a constant $c>0$ independent of all relevant parameters such that $A\\leq cB$.\nL87: ### 2.1 The impossibility theorem for equispaced points\nL88: \nL89: We first review the impossibility theorem of [cite30†27 ]. Let $\\{x_{m}\\}^{M}_{m=0}=\\{-1+2m/M\\}^{M}_{m=0}$ be a grid of $M+1$ equispaced points in $[-1,1]$ and suppose that $F_{M}:C([-1,1])\\rightarrow C([-1,1])$ is a family of mappings such that $F_{M}(f)$ depends only on the values of $f$ on this grid. We define the (absolute) condition numbers as\nL90:  | $$\\kappa(F_{M})=\\sup_{f\\in C([-1,1])}\\lim_{\\delta\\rightarrow 0^{+}}\\sup_{\\begin{subarray}{c}h\\in C([-1,1])\\\\\nL91: 0<\\|h\\|_{M,\\infty}\\leq\\delta\\end{subarray}}\\frac{\\|F_{M}(f+h)-F_{M}(f)\\|_{\\infty}}{\\|h\\|_{M,\\infty}}.$$  |  | (2.1)\nL92: \nL93: Suppose that $E\\subseteq\\mathbb{C}$ is a compact set. We now write $B(E)$ for the Banach space of functions that are continuous on $E$ and analytic in its interior with norm $\\|f\\|_{E}=\\sup_{z\\in E}|f(z)|$.\nL94: ###### Theorem 2.1 ([cite30†27 ]).\nL95: \nL96: Let $E\\subseteq\\mathbb{C}$ be a compact set containing $[-1,1]$ in its interior and suppose that $\\{F_{M}\\}^{\\infty}_{M=1}$ is an approximation procedure based on equispaced grids of $M+1$ points such that for some $C,\\rho>1$ and $1/2<\\tau\\leq 1$ we have\nL97: \nL98:  | $$\\|f-F_{M}(f)\\|_{\\infty}\\leq C\\rho^{-M^{\\tau}}\\|f\\|_{E},\\hskip 20.00003ptM=1,2,\\ldots,$$  |\nL99: \nL100: for all $f\\in B(E)$. Then the condition numbers (cite50†2.1 ) satisfy\nL101: \nL102:  | $$\\kappa(F_{M})\\geq\\sigma^{M^{2\\tau-1}},$$  |\nL103: for some $\\sigma>1$ and all large $M$.\nL104: \nL105: Specializing to $\\tau=1$, this theorem states that exponential convergence at a geometric rate implies exponential ill-conditioning at a geometric rate. Conversely, stability of any method is only possible when $\\tau=1/2$, which corresponds to root-exponential convergence in $M$.\nL106: ### 2.2 Coppersmith & Rivlin’s bound\nL107: \nL108: The proof of Theorem cite51†2.1 , although it does not pertain to polynomials or polynomial approximation specifically, relies on a result of Coppersmith & Rivlin on the maximal behaviour of polynomials bounded on an equispaced grid. To state this result, we first introduce the following notation:\nL109: \nL110:  | $$B(M,N)=\\sup\\left\\{\\|p\\|_{\\infty}:p\\in\\mathbb{P}_{N},\\|p\\|_{M,\\infty}\\leq 1\\right\\}.$$  |  | (2.2)\nL111: \nL112: Note that in the special case $M=N$, this is just the Lebesgue constant\nL113:  | $$B(N,N)=\\Lambda(N)=\\sup\\left\\{\\|F_{N}(f)\\|_{\\infty}:f\\in C([-1,1]),\\|f\\|_{\\infty}\\leq 1\\right\\},$$  |\nL114: \nL115: where $F_{N}(f)$ denotes the polynomial interpolant of degree $N$ of a function $f$.\nL116: ###### Theorem 2.2 ([cite39†16 ]).\nL117: \nL118: Let $\\{x_{m}\\}^{M}_{m=0}=\\{-1+2m/M\\}^{M}_{m=0}$ be an equispaced grid of $M+1$ points in $[-1,1]$ and suppose that $1\\leq N\\leq M$. Then there exist constants $b\\geq a>1$ such that\nL119: \nL120:  | $$a^{N^{2}/M}\\leq B(M,N)\\leq b^{N^{2}/M}.$$  |\nL121: Two implications of this result are as follows. First, a polynomial of degree $N$ bounded on $M=\\mathcal{O}\\left(N\\right)$ equispaced points can grow at most exponentially large in between those points. Second, one needs quadratically-many equispaced points, i.e. $M\\asymp N^{2}$, in order to prohibit growth of an arbitrary polynomial of degree $N$ that is bounded on an equispaced grid.\nL122: We remark in passing that when $M=N$, so that $B(N,N)=\\Lambda(N)$ is the Lebesgue constant, one also has the well-known estimate $\\Lambda(N)\\sim\\frac{2^{N+1}}{\\mathrm{e}N\\log(N)}$ for large $N$ (see, for example, [cite52†31 , Chpt. 15]).\nL123: Sufficiency of the scaling $M\\asymp N^{2}$ is a much older result than Theorem cite53†2.2 , dating back to work Schönhage [cite44†30 ] and Ehlich & Zeller [cite54†20 , cite55†21 ] in the 1960s. Ehlich also proved unboundedness of $B(M,N)$ if $M=o(N^{2})$ as $N\\rightarrow\\infty$ [cite56†19 ]. More recently, Rakhmanov [cite57†29 ] has given a very precise analysis of not just $B(M,N)$ but also the pointwise quantity $B(M,N,x)=\\sup\\{|p(x)|:p\\in\\mathbb{P}_{N},\\|p\\|_{M,\\infty}\\leq 1\\}$ for $-1\\leq x\\leq 1$.\nL124: ### 2.3 Discrete least squares\nL125: \nL126: A simple algorithm that attains the bounds implied by Theorem cite51†2.1 is discrete least-squares fitting with polynomials:\nL127: \nL128:  | $$F_{M,N}(f)=\\underset{p\\in\\mathbb{P}_{N}}{\\operatorname{argmin}}\\sum^{M}_{m=0}|f(x_{m})-p(x_{m})|^{2}.$$  |  | (2.3)\nL129: Here $N\\leq M$ is a parameter which is chosen to ensure specific rates of convergence. The following result determines the conditioning and convergence of this approximation (note that this result is valid for arbitrary sets of points, not just equispaced):\nL130: ###### Proposition 2.4.\nL131: \nL132: Let $\\{x_{m}\\}^{M}_{m=0}$ be a set of $M+1$ points in $[-1,1]$ and suppose that $1\\leq N\\leq M$. If $f\\in C([-1,1])$ and $F_{M,N}(f)$ is as in (cite58†2.3 ) then the error\nL133: \nL134:  | $$\\|f-F_{M,N}(f)\\|_{\\infty}\\leq(1+\\kappa_{M,N})\\inf_{p\\in\\mathbb{P}_{N}}\\|f-p\\|_{\\infty},$$  |\nL135: \nL136: where $\\kappa_{M,N}=\\kappa(F_{M,N})$ is the condition number of $F_{M,N}$. Moreover,\nL137: \nL138:  | $$B(M,N)\\leq\\kappa_{M,N}\\leq\\sqrt{M+1}B(M,N),$$  |  | (2.4)\nL139: where $B(M,N)$ is as in (cite59†2.2 ). Furthermore, if $M=N$, i.e. $F_{N}=F_{N,N}$ is the polynomial interpolant of degree $N$, then\nL140: \nL141:  | $$\\kappa_{N,N}=B(N,N)=\\Lambda(N),$$  |  | (2.5)\nL142: \nL143: is the Lebesgue constant.\nL144: \nL145: Although this result is well known, we include a short proof for completeness:\nL146: ###### Proof.\nL147: \nL148: Since the points are distinct and $N\\leq M$, the least-squares solution exists uniquely. Notice that the mapping $F_{M,N}$ is linear and a projection onto $\\mathbb{P}_{N}$. Hence\nL149: \nL150:  | $$\\kappa_{M,N}=\\sup_{\\begin{subarray}{c}f\\in C([-1,1])\\\\\nL151: \\|f\\|_{M,\\infty}\\neq 0\\end{subarray}}\\frac{\\|F_{M,N}(f)\\|_{\\infty}}{\\|f\\|_{M,\\infty}},$$  |  | (2.6)\nL152: \nL153: and consequently we have\nL154:  | $$\\|f-F_{M,N}(f)\\|_{\\infty}\\leq\\|f-p\\|_{\\infty}+\\|F_{M,N}(f-p)\\|_{\\infty}\\leq(1+\\kappa_{M,N})\\|f-p\\|_{\\infty},\\hskip 10.00002pt\\forall p\\in\\mathbb{P}_{N}.$$  |\nL155: \nL156: It remains to estimate the condition number. Because $F_{M,N}(f)$ is a polynomial, it follows that\nL157: \nL158:  | $$\\kappa_{M,N}\\leq B(M,N)\\sup_{\\begin{subarray}{c}f\\in C([-1,1])\\\\\nL159: \\|f\\|_{M,\\infty}\\neq 0\\end{subarray}}\\frac{\\|F_{M,N}(f)\\|_{M,\\infty}}{\\|f\\|_{M,\\infty}}.$$  |  | (2.7)\nL160: \nL161: Now observe that\nL162:  | $$\\|F_{M,N}(f)\\|^{2}_{M,\\infty}\\leq\\sum^{M}_{m=0}|F_{M,N}(f)(x_{m})|^{2}=\\frac{M+1}{2}\\|F_{M,N}(f)\\|^{2}_{M,2}.$$  |\nL163: Since $F_{M,N}(f)$ is the solution of a discrete least-squares problem it is a projection with respect to the discrete semi-inner product $\\langle\\cdot,\\cdot\\rangle_{M}$. Hence $\\|F_{M,N}(f)\\|_{M,2}\\leq\\|f\\|_{M,2}\\leq\\sqrt{2}\\|f\\|_{\\infty}$. Combining this with the previous estimate gives the upper bound $\\kappa_{M,N}\\leq\\sqrt{M+1}B(M,N)$. For the lower bound, we use (cite60†2.6 ) and the fact that $F_{M,N}$ is a projection to deduce that\nL164:  | $$\\kappa_{M,N}\\geq\\max_{\\begin{subarray}{c}p\\in\\mathbb{P}_{N}\\\\\nL165: \\|p\\|_{M,\\infty}\\neq 0\\end{subarray}}\\frac{\\|p\\|_{\\infty}}{\\|p\\|_{M,\\infty}}=B(M,N).$$  |\nL166: \nL167: This completes the proof of (cite61†2.4 ). For (cite62†2.5 ) we merely use the definition of $\\Lambda(N)$. ∎\nL168: ### 2.4 Examples of nonequispaced points\nL169: \nL170: To illustrate our main results proved later in the paper, we shall consider points $-1=x_{0}<x_{1}<\\ldots<x_{M}=1$ that are equidistributed with respect to so-called modified Jacobi weight functions. These are defined as\nL171: \nL172:  | $$\\mu(x)=g(x)(1-x)^{\\alpha}(1+x)^{\\beta},$$  |  | (2.8)\nL173: \nL174: where $\\alpha,\\beta>-1$ and $g\\in L^{\\infty}(-1,1)$ satisfies $c_{1}\\leq g(x)\\leq c_{2}$ almost everywhere. Throughout, we assume the normalization\nL175:  | $$\\int^{1}_{-1}\\mu(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x=1,$$  |\nL176: \nL177: in which case the points $\\{x_{m}\\}^{M}_{m=0}$ are defined implicitly by\nL178: \nL179:  | $$\\frac{m}{M}=\\int^{x_{m}}_{-1}\\mu(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\\hskip 10.00002ptm=0,\\ldots,M.$$  |  | (2.9)\nL180: \nL181: Ultraspherical weight functions are special cases of modified Jacobi weight functions. They are defined as\nL182: \nL183:  | $$\\mu(x)=c(1-x^{2})^{\\alpha},\\hskip 20.00003ptc=\\left(\\int^{1}_{-1}(1-x^{2})^{\\alpha}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\right)^{-1},$$  |  | (2.10)\nL184: for $\\alpha>-1$. Within this subclass, we shall consider a number of specific examples:\nL185: \nL186:   * (U)\nL187: \nL188: ($\\alpha=0$) The uniform weight function $\\mu(x)=\\frac{1}{2}$, corresponding to the equispaced points $x_{m}=-1+2\\frac{m}{M}$.\nL189: \nL190:   * (C1)\nL191: ($\\alpha=-1/2$) The Chebyshev weight function of the first kind $\\mu(x)=\\frac{1}{\\pi\\sqrt{1-x^{2}}}$, corresponding to the Chebyshev points. Note that these points are roughly equispaced near $x=0$ and quadratically spaced near the endpoints $x=\\pm 1$. That is, $|x_{1}+1|,|x_{M-1}-1|=\\mathcal{O}\\left(M^{-2}\\right)$.\nL192: \nL193:   * (C2)\nL194: ($\\alpha=\\frac{1}{2}$) The Chebyshev weight function of the second kind $\\mu(x)=\\frac{2}{\\pi}\\sqrt{1-x^{2}}$. Note that the corresponding points are roughly equispaced near $x=0$, but are sparse near the endpoints. In particular, $|x_{1}+1|,|x_{M-1}-1|=\\mathcal{O}\\left(M^{-1/2}\\right)$.\nL195: Recall that for (U) one requires a quadratic scaling $M\\asymp N^{2}$ to ensure stability. Conversely, for (C1) any linear scaling of $M=cN$ with $c>1$ suffices (see Remark cite15†3.2 ). Since the points (C2) are so poorly distributed near the endpoints, we expect, and it will turn out to be the case, that a more severe scaling than quadratic is required for stability in this case.\nL196: \nL197: We shall also consider two further examples:\nL198: \nL199:   * (UC)\nL200: ($\\alpha=-1/4$) The corresponding points cluster at the endpoints, although not quadratically. Specifically, $|x_{1}+1|,|x_{M-1}-1|=\\mathcal{O}\\left(M^{-4/3}\\right).$\nL201: \nL202:   * (OC)\nL203: \nL204: ($\\alpha=-3/4$) The corresponding points overcluster at the endpoints: $|x_{1}+1|,|x_{M-1}-1|=\\mathcal{O}\\left(M^{-4}\\right)$.\nL205: We expect (UC) to require a superlinear scaling of $M$ with $N$ for stability, although not as severe as quadratic scaling as in the case of (U). Conversely, in (OC) it transpires that linear scaling suffices, but unlike the case of (C1), the scaling factor $c$ (where $M/N=c$) must be sufficiently large.\nL206: \nL207: The node clustering for the above distributions is illustrated in Figure cite63†1 . This figure also shows the corresponding cumulative distribution functions $\\int_{-1}^{x}\\mu(\\xi)d\\xi$.\nL208: C2            UC            C1            OC\nL209: \nL210: cite64†Image: Refer to caption L211: \nL212: Figure 1: Relationship between $m/M$ and $x_{m}$ given by (cite65†2.9 ) for the ultraspherical weight functions with $\\alpha=1/2$ (C2), $\\alpha=-1/4$ (UC), $\\alpha=-1/2$ (C1), and $\\alpha=-3/4$ (OC). Here $M=10$.\nL213: ## 3 Maximal behaviour of polynomials bounded on arbitrary grids\nL214: \nL215: We now seek to estimate the maximal behaviour of a polynomial of degree $N$ that is bounded at arbitrary nodes $-1=x_{0}<x_{1}<\\ldots<x_{M}=1$. As in §cite10†2.2 , we define\nL216: \nL217:  | $$B(M,N)=\\sup\\left\\{\\|p\\|_{\\infty}:p\\in\\mathbb{P}_{N},\\ |p(x_{m})|\\leq 1,\\ m=0,\\ldots,M\\right\\}.$$  |  | (3.1)\nL218: \nL219: Once again we note that $B(N,N)=\\Lambda(N)$ is the Lebesgue constant of polynomial interpolation.\nL220: ### 3.1 Lower bound for $B(M,N)$\nL221: \nL222: Our first main result of the paper is a generalization of the lower bound of Coppersmith & Rivlin (Theorem cite53†2.2 ) to arbitrary nodes. Before stating this, we need several definitions. First, given $M\\geq N\\geq 1$ and nodes $-1=x_{0}<x_{1}<\\ldots<x_{M}=1$, we define\nL223:  | $\\displaystyle Q_{-}(K,N)$  | $\\displaystyle=\\frac{\\pi}{8}\\left(\\frac{2N^{2}}{\\pi^{2}}\\right)^{K-1}\\frac{1}{\\Gamma(K+1/2)^{2}}\\prod^{K-1}_{n=1}(1+x_{n}),\\hskip 20.00003pt2\\leq K\\leq N,$  |\n"

